  "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{1}{5}>0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-9$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-9+1}{2}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-4$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+9)(x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-4+9)(-4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-4,-5),$ and the range of the function is $[-5,\\infty ),$ i.e. $-5\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/97c587abebf09ff27a805db3b32b3c5f26b5d51baaf7f6827618e01ccdd53642.png)",
